      "writtenOn": "2022-06-03T08:04:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is generally not recommended to use fixed port numbers in tests. Some other process might be sitting on the same port, and the bind could fail. Or, for a tcp socket, the bind might fail with EADDRINUSE because there\u0027s a time_wait socket on that port.\n\nInstead suggest:\n\n1. Create the server socket and ask the kernel to bind to a free port.\n2. Get the port number from the socket.\n3. Use that port number in the client socket.\n\nAs for how to do #1: with the sockets API, just bind to 0 and the kernel will pick a free ephemeral port for you; with DatagramSocket I\u0027m not sure. But also note you can use the sockets API from java code by using the android.system.Os package. Something like:\n\n    FileDescriptor sock \u003d Os.socket(AF_INET6, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);\n\nThen you can use Os.bind, Os.connect, etc. I find these more intuitive and easier to use than java Socket and especially DatagramSocket.\n\nFor now this is probably fine since UDP does not have time-wait sockets. Also while, 5555 would be a very bad choice for a TCP port since it\u0027s the default port used by ADB, but ADB uses TCP, not UDP.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3319,
        "startChar": 42,
        "endLine": 3319,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "98fc5d82737ff837cf2af44bdd804f81d570558d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
