      "writtenOn": "2022-06-03T08:04:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This will affect the actual state of networking on the device after the test: all deny firewall chains will be disabled until something re-enables them. This might cause unexpected behaviour of the device, and might also cause other higher-level code to be confused, or tests for that higher-level code (e.g., CtsHostsideNetworkTests) to fail.\n\nWhat we do in other tests that affect global device state is fetch the current value before running the test, then run the test, then restore the previous value. It would be good to do that here too, but unfortunately I don\u0027t think we have a way to get the state of a firewall chain.\n\nMaybe in a follow-up CL we could add an @hide getFirewallChainEnabled(int chain) method in ConnectivityManager that you could call from this test?\n\nYou could implement it pretty simply in BpfNetMaps without JNI code by just opening the map using the Java BpfMap class and reading the value.\n\n\nFor now, maybe just test the two new OEM deny rules? That will minimize the risk of causing other tests to break. Hopefully by the time this code makes it out to OEMs we\u0027ll have added a getFirewallChainEnabled so this never impacts their testing.\n\nGiven that we want to get this change in ASAP, maybe just add a TODO to test the other rules, or even comment out the code that tests them?",
      "range": {
